.TH MOUNT 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
mount \- list or mount filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount
.br
.B mount
.I source dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B mount
lists what is mounted, sorted by path, one a line:
.PP
.nf
\fIsource\fR on \fIpath\fR type \fItype\fR
.fi
.PP
The types are
.B hfs
(a disk),
.B tmpfs
(a filesystem in memory) and
.BR devfs .
.PP
With two arguments,
.B mount
mounts
.I source
on
.IR dir .
.I source
is a disk's bus address (its slot path, as
.BR sata:1 ,
or its id,
.BI id: uuid ;
see
.BR lsdev (1)),
which must be a storage device in hfs mode; or
.B tmpfs
for a new, empty filesystem in memory; or
.B devfs
for the device files.
.I dir
need not exist: a mount point shows up as a directory.
.PP
The kernel mounts the boot disk on
.IR / ,
devfs on
.IR /dev ,
a tmpfs on
.IR /tmp ,
and every other hfs disk on the bus under
.I /mnt
.RI ( /mnt/sata/1
for
.BR sata:1 )
by itself, as disks come and go.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 with the wrong number of arguments, or if the mount
failed (something is mounted there already: busy; not a storage device:
no such device; a raw disk: invalid argument).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mount sata:2 /mnt/backup
mount tmpfs /scratch
.fi
.SH NOTES
There are no options and no
.IR /etc/fstab ;
a filesystem type cannot be given apart from the source.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umount (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR lsdev (1)
